<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franco-Dutch War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1672">1672</a>–<a href="/years/1678">1678</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Dutch War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Franco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> War was fought from 1672 to 1678 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its allies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, later supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s invasion in 1672 nearly destroyed the Republic, which survived by flooding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-water-line">Holland Water Line</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaties of Nijmegen</a> in 1678 left the Dutch territory intact, while France gained Franche-Comté and towns in the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war.jpg" alt="Franco-Dutch War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DavidDijkgraaf · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franco-Dutch_War_Collave.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> during the Eighty Years War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-munster">Peace of Münster</a> of 1648 confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> independence and closed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> estuary, which benefited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> by removing its rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. After the death of William II of Orange in 1650, political control passed to the urban patricians, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regenten">Regenten</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-de-witt">Johan de Witt</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a> from 1653 to 1672. The States of Holland refused to support a division of the Spanish Netherlands, which convinced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> that his aims could only be reached by force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The French offensive began on 4 May 1672, with Turenne's main army of 50,000 and a second force under Condé. By 5 June the French had taken Rheinberg, Orsoy and Burick with little resistance, and they crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, fighting the Battle of Tolhuis on 12 June. William of Orange withdrew behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-water-line">Holland Water Line</a>, the flooding of which had been ordered only on 8 June. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> remember 1672 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rampjaar">Rampjaar</a>, the Disaster Year. Rioting in the cities of Holland replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regenten">Regenten</a> with Orangist partisans, and French peace terms of 1 July caused outrage.</p><p>The position stabilised by late July. Emperor Leopold I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> side, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> left the war in February 1674. In 1673 the French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>, which surrendered on 30 June. In 1674 they overran Franche-Comté in under six weeks, and Turenne won a winter campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> that ended in January 1675. William was halted by Condé at the Battle of Seneffe. Sweden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s ally, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and was beaten at Fehrbellin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Neither side won a decisive victory. By 1677 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> held Franche-Comté and had made gains in the Spanish Netherlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, helped by victory at the Battle of Cassel and by Vauban's line of fortresses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Peace of Nijmegen</a> of September 1678 returned Charleroi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, while France received all of Franche-Comté and towns including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> and Saint-Omer. All but Ypres remain French. The terms were worse than those on offer in July 1672.</p><p>Louis came to be called the Sun King in the years after the war. William of Orange became dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> domestic politics, and this helped him build the anti-French Grand Alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> (1688-1697) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> (1701-1714).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franco-Dutch War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Franco-Dutch War</b> began in 1672 and funding was cut until 1674.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Franco-Dutch War</b>, the French army under Marshal Turenne made a surprise winter march through the Vosges and defeated the imperial and Brandenburg forces near Colmar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory secured the supremacy of the French fleet for the rest of the <b>Franco-Dutch War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Fought 23 days after the Treaty of Westminster, it was likely the last battle of the Third Anglo-<b>Dutch War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-14">14 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
